{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_42","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luiz_In%C3%A1cio_Lula_da_Silva"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luiz_In%C3%A1cio_Lula_da_Silva:\n| [His Excellency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Excellency \"Excellency\")  Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva | |\n| --- | --- |\n| [Head shot of Lula smiling](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Foto_oficial_de_Luiz_In%C3%A1cio_Lula_da_Silva_(ombros)_denoise_(cropped).jpg) Lula in 2023 | |\n|  | |\n| 35th & 39th [President of Brazil](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Brazil \"President of Brazil\") | |\n| **[Incumbent](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incumbent \"Incumbent\")** | |\n| **Assumed office**  1 January 2023 | |\n| Vice President | [Geraldo Alckmin](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geraldo_Alckmin \"Geraldo Alckmin\") |\n| Preceded by | [Jair Bolsonaro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jair_Bolsonaro \"Jair Bolsonaro\") |\n| **In office** 1 January 2003 – 1 January 2011 | |\n| Vice President | [José Alencar](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jos%C3%A9_Alencar \"José Alencar\") |\n| Preceded by | [Fernando Henrique Cardoso](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fernando_Henrique_Cardoso \"Fernando Henrique Cardoso\") |\n| Succeeded by | [Dilma Rousseff](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dilma_Rousseff \"Dilma Rousseff\") |\n| | Legislative and Party offices | | | --- | --- | |  | |  | | | National President of the [Workers' Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Workers'_Party_(Brazil) \"Workers' Party (Brazil)\") | | | **In office** 15 July 1990 – 24 January 1994 | | | Preceded by | [Luiz Gushiken](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luiz_Gushiken \"Luiz Gushiken\") | | Succeeded by | [Rui Falcão](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rui_Falc%C3%A3o \"Rui Falcão\") | | **In office** 9 August 1980 – 17 January 1988 | | | Preceded by | *Office established* | | Succeeded by | [Olívio Dutra](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ol%C3%ADvio_Dutra \"Olívio Dutra\") | | Member of the [Chamber of Deputies](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamber_of_Deputies_(Brazil) \"Chamber of Deputies (Brazil)\") | | | **In office** 1 February 1987 – 1 February 1991[[a]](#cite_note-1) | | | Constituency | [São Paulo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%A3o_Paulo_(state) \"São Paulo (state)\") | | |\n|  | |\n| | Union leadership positions | | | --- | --- | |  | |  | | | President of the [ABC Metalworkers' Union](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ABC_Metalworkers'_Union \"ABC Metalworkers' Union\")[[b]](#cite_note-2) | | | **In office** 19 April 1975 – 18 April 1981 | | | Preceded by | Paulo Vidal Neto | | Succeeded by | [Jair Meneguelli](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jair_Meneguelli \"Jair Meneguelli\") | | First Secretary of the ABC Metalworkers' Union | | | **In office** c. April 1972 – 18 April 1975 | | | President | Paulo Vidal Neto | | Preceded by | *Unknown* | | Succeeded by | Paulo Vidal Neto | | Alternate Director of the ABC Metalworkers' Union | | | **In office** c. April 1969 – c. April 1972 | | | President | Paulo Vidal Neto | | |\n|  | |\n|  | |\n| Personal details | |\n| Born | Luiz Inácio da Silva 27 October 1945 (age 80)  [Garanhuns](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Garanhuns \"Garanhuns\"), Pernambuco, Brazil |\n| [Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Political_party \"Political party\") | [PT](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Workers'_Party_(Brazil) \"Workers' Party (Brazil)\") (since 1980) |\n| Other political affiliations | [FE Brasil](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazil_of_Hope \"Brazil of Hope\") (since 2022) |\n| Spouses | Maria de Lourdes Ribeiro  ​  ​  (m. ; died   )​      [Marisa Letícia Casa](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marisa_Let%C3%ADcia_Lula_da_Silva \"Marisa Letícia Lula da Silva\")  ​  ​  (m.   ; died   )​      [Rosângela da Silva](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ros%C3%A2ngela_Lula_da_Silva \"Rosângela Lula da Silva\")  ​  (m.   )​ |\n| Children | 5 |\n| Education | [National Service for Industrial Training](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SENAI \"SENAI\") |\n| Occupation | * [Metalworker](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metalworker \"Metalworker\") * trade unionist |\n| Signature | [Lula (Signature of Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Signature_of_Luiz_In%C3%A1cio_Lula_da_Silva.svg \"Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva's signature\") |\n| Website | [lula.com.br](http://lula.com.br/) |\n| Lula da Silva on [the taxation of Brazilian products](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tariffs_in_the_second_Trump_administration#Brazil \"Tariffs in the second Trump administration\") by the Trump administration Recorded 17 July 2025 | |\n|  | |\n\n**Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva**[[c]](#cite_note-3) (born **Luiz Inácio da Silva**; 27 October 1945), known [mononymously](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mononym \"Mononym\") as **Lula**, is a Brazilian politician, trade unionist and former metalworker who has served as [president of Brazil](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/President_of_Brazil \"President of Brazil\") since 2023 and from 2003 to 2011. He is a member of the [Workers' Party](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Workers'_Party_(Brazil) \"Workers' Party (Brazil)\").\n\nBorn in [Pernambuco](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pernambuco \"Pernambuco\"), Lula quit school after second grade to work, and did not learn to read until he was ten years old. As a teenager, he worked as a metalworker and became a [trade unionist](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Trade_union \"Trade union\"). Between 1978 and 1980, he led the [ABC workers' strikes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1978%E2%80%931980_ABC_Paulista_strikes \"1978–1980 ABC Paulista strikes\") during Brazil's [military dictatorship](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_dictatorship_in_Brazil \"Military dictatorship in Brazil\"), and in 1980, he helped start the Workers' Party during [Brazil's redemocratization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Redemocratization_in_Brazil \"Redemocratization in Brazil\"). Lula was one of the leaders of the 1984 *[Diretas Já](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Diretas_J%C3%A1 \"Diretas Já\")* movement, which demanded direct elections. In [1986](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1986_Brazilian_legislative_election \"1986 Brazilian legislative election\"), he was elected a federal deputy in the state of [São Paulo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S%C3%A3o_Paulo_(state) \"São Paulo (state)\"). He ran for president in [1989](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1989_Brazilian_presidential_election \"1989 Brazilian presidential election\"), but lost in the second round. He also lost presidential elections in [1994](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1994_Brazilian_general_election \"1994 Brazilian general election\") and [1998](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1998_Brazilian_general_election \"1998 Brazilian general election\"). He finally became president in [2002](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2002_Brazilian_presidential_election \"2002 Brazilian presidential election\"), in a runoff. In [2006](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2006_Brazilian_presidential_election \"2006 Brazilian presidential election\"), he was successfully re-elected in the second round.\n\nDescribed as left-wing, his [first presidency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_presidency_of_Lula_da_Silva \"First presidency of Lula da Silva\") coincided with South America's first [pink tide](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink_tide \"Pink tide\"). During his first two consecutive terms in office, he continued fiscal policies and promoted social welfare programs such as [Bolsa Família](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bolsa_Fam%C3%ADlia \"Bolsa Família\") that eventually led to GDP growth, reduction in external debt and inflation, and helping millions of Brazilians escape poverty. He also played a role in foreign policy, both on a regional level and as part of global trade and environment negotiations.[[1]](#cite_note-4) During those terms, Lula was considered one of the most popular politicians in Brazil's history and left office with 80% approval rating.[[2]](#cite_note-auto6-5)[[3]](#cite_note-6)[[4]](#cite_note-7) His first term was also marked by notable corruption scandals, including the [Mensalão vote-buying scandal](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mensal%C3%A3o_scandal \"Mensalão scandal\"). After the [2010 Brazilian general election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2010_Brazilian_general_election \"2010 Brazilian general election\"), he was succeeded by his former chief of staff, [Dilma Rousseff](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dilma_Rousseff \"Dilma Rousseff\"), and remained active in politics and gave lectures.\n\nIn July 2017, Lula was convicted on charges of [money laundering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Money_laundering \"Money laundering\") and corruption in the [Operation Car Wash](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Operation_Car_Wash \"Operation Car Wash\") context. He was [arrested in April 2018](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Imprisonment_of_Luiz_In%C3%A1cio_Lula_da_Silva \"Imprisonment of Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva\") and went on to spend a total of 580 days in prison. Lula attempted to run in the [2018 Brazilian presidential election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_Brazilian_presidential_election \"2018 Brazilian presidential election\"), but was disqualified under Brazil's *[Ficha Limpa](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ficha_Limpa \"Ficha Limpa\")* law. He was convicted again in February 2019, and was released from prison the following November. His two convictions were nullified in 2021 by the [Supreme Federal Court](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supreme_Federal_Court \"Supreme Federal Court\"), in a ruling which also found serious biases in the first case against him, also annulling all other pending cases. Once legally allowed to make another run for the presidency, Lula did so in the [2022 election](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Brazilian_general_election \"2022 Brazilian general election\") and ultimately defeated the incumbent [Jair Bolsonaro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jair_Bolsonaro \"Jair Bolsonaro\") in a runoff. Sworn in on 1 January 2023 at the age of 77, he became the oldest Brazilian president at time of inauguration, as well as the first to have defeated an incumbent president and to be elected to a third term.\n\nDuring his [second presidency](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Lula_da_Silva \"Second presidency of Lula da Silva\"), Lula faced the [8 January \n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 10000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
