{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_51","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":10000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yanomami_humanitarian_crisis"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yanomami_humanitarian_crisis:\nYanomami humanitarian crisis\n\n|  |  |\n| --- | --- |\n| Duration | 2019–2023 |\n| Location | * Yanomami Indigenous Territory, Brazil |\n| Type | Mass deaths, famine, [forced displacements](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forced_displacements \"Forced displacements\") and other major human rights violations |\n\nDuring the [presidency of Jair Bolsonaro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Presidency_of_Jair_Bolsonaro \"Presidency of Jair Bolsonaro\") (2019–2022) and the [second presidency of Lula da Silva](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Second_presidency_of_Lula_da_Silva \"Second presidency of Lula da Silva\") (2023), a series of [mass deaths](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genocide_of_indigenous_peoples \"Genocide of indigenous peoples\"), famine, [forced displacements](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Forced_displacement \"Forced displacement\") and other major [human rights violations](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Human_rights \"Human rights\") took place in the Brazilian [Yanomami Indigenous Territory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yanomami_Indigenous_Territory \"Yanomami Indigenous Territory\").[[1]](#cite_note-1)[[2]](#cite_note-2)[[3]](#cite_note-3) Such events reportedly started or were aggravated from 2019 on as a consequence of rampant [exploitation of natural resources](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Exploitation_of_natural_resources \"Exploitation of natural resources\") by individuals and companies with state approval, and have been frequently said to constitute a [genocide](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genocide \"Genocide\") against the [Yanomami people](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yanomami \"Yanomami\").[[4]](#cite_note-4)[[5]](#cite_note-5)[[6]](#cite_note-6) It is part of the larger ongoing [genocide of Indigenous peoples in Brazil](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genocide_of_Indigenous_peoples_in_Brazil \"Genocide of Indigenous peoples in Brazil\").\n\nAfter being elected in the [2018 Brazilian general elections](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2018_Brazilian_general_election \"2018 Brazilian general election\") and taking office under his pledge to loosen environmental policies, especially in the [Brazilian Amazon](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazilian_Amazon \"Brazilian Amazon\") region, then president [Jair Bolsonaro](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jair_Bolsonaro \"Jair Bolsonaro\") repealed several presidential decrees banning [illegal mining](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illegal_mining \"Illegal mining\") and [illegal logging](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illegal_logging \"Illegal logging\") nationwide, and effectively dismantled environment protection agencies.[[7]](#cite_note-7)[[8]](#cite_note-8)[[9]](#cite_note-9)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/21.01.2023_-_An%C3%BAncio_de_a%C3%A7%C3%B5es_emergenciais_para_a_popula%C3%A7%C3%A3o_Yanomami_%2852640432047%29.jpg/330px-21.01.2023_-_An%C3%BAncio_de_a%C3%A7%C3%B5es_emergenciais_para_a_popula%C3%A7%C3%A3o_Yanomami_%2852640432047%29.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:21.01.2023_-_An%C3%BAncio_de_a%C3%A7%C3%B5es_emergenciais_para_a_popula%C3%A7%C3%A3o_Yanomami_(52640432047).jpg)\n\nPresident [Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luiz_In%C3%A1cio_Lula_da_Silva \"Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva\") announces emergency measures to tackle the Yanomami humanitarian crisis.\n\nIn January 2023, following the end of Bolsonaro's administration due to his defeat in the [2022 Brazilian general elections](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Brazilian_general_election \"2022 Brazilian general election\"), new government officials appointed by President [Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luiz_In%C3%A1cio_Lula_da_Silva \"Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva\") took office and immediately were briefed about an escalation of the Yanomami crisis.[[10]](#cite_note-10) Over 20,000 illegal miners were estimated to have invaded and exploited the Yanomami reservation, prompting comparisons with the 1980's [Serra Pelada gold rush](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Serra_Pelada \"Serra Pelada\"), also in the Amazon.[[11]](#cite_note-11)[[12]](#cite_note-12)\n\nGovernment neglect, agricultural encroachment and illegal activities affecting the area preceded the creation of the Yanomami reserve in 1992. The first contacts between the Yanomami indigenous peoples and white men intermittently happened between the 1910s and 1940s; in the following two decades, such contacts increased due to [religious missions](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Missionary \"Missionary\") in the region, road construction work and mining projects by the [military regime](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Military_dictatorship_in_Brazil \"Military dictatorship in Brazil\") in the area in the 1970s, during which the first reports of epidemics especially of [flu](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Influenza \"Influenza\"), [measles](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Measles \"Measles\") and [whooping cough](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whooping_cough \"Whooping cough\") emerged linked to the decimation of entire Yanomami communities.[[13]](#cite_note-13) Ever since, the region has suffered from being a hotspot for illegal activities, notably [illegal mining](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illegal_mining \"Illegal mining\"), leading to [mercury poisoning](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mercury_poisoning \"Mercury poisoning\") of several tribespeople, including [infants](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Infant \"Infant\").[[14]](#cite_note-14)\n\n### Illegal mining logistics\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Yanomami_humanitarian_crisis&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Illegal mining logistics\")]\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Yanomami_location.png/330px-Yanomami_location.png)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Yanomami_location.png)\n\nThe [Yanomami indigenous territory](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yanomami_Indigenous_Territory \"Yanomami Indigenous Territory\") location.\n\nA network of politicians, local public servants, farmers, business people and companies was organized to illegally exploit [gold](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold \"Gold\") and [cassiterite](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cassiterite \"Cassiterite\") as well as to [launder](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Money_laundering \"Money laundering\") the criminal activities in the Yanomami territory. Hidden [runaways](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Runway \"Runway\") were built in dozens of nearby and even distant rural properties (as far as in [Boa Vista](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boa_Vista,_Roraima \"Boa Vista, Roraima\")). Some of the gold extracted has been taken to Venezuela and French Guyana, and [refinery facilities](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Refinery \"Refinery\") and depots have also been used in the scheme.[[15]](#cite_note-www1.folha.uol.com.br-15)\n\nAdditionally, Rodrigo Martins de Mello, a pro-Bolsonaro businessman, was charged with operating under an \"irregular license\"[[16]](#cite_note-16) and heading another [criminal organization](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Criminal_organization \"Criminal organization\") operating in the reservation.[[17]](#cite_note-17)\n\nOn January 16, 2023, Brazilian newspaper *[Folha de São Paulo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Folha_de_S.Paulo \"Folha de S.Paulo\")* reported that Brazilian former army general [Augusto Heleno](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Augusto_Heleno \"Augusto Heleno\"), a former minister under the [Bolsonaro government](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bolsonaro_government \"Bolsonaro government\"), authorized a convicted drug dealer to run a gold mining project in the Yanomami region.[[18]](#cite_note-18) On January 27, Brazilian newspaper *[O Globo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/O_Globo \"O Globo\")* reported that the [Brazilian Central Bank](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central_Bank_of_Brazil \"Central Bank of Brazil\") failed to crack down on [gold laundering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gold_laundering \"Gold laundering\"). The failure has reportedly emboldened some 20,000 illegal miners to further exploit indigenous territories including the Yanomami reservation.[[19]](#cite_note-19)\n\nLegal mining companies such as M.M. Gold (rebranded from Gana Gold) have also masqueraded illegal activities by faking far more than permitted amounts of gold to be extracted in their concessions, according to a report by *[Mongabay](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mongabay \"Mongabay\")* and [*The Intercept* *Brazil*](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Intercept_Brasil \"The Intercept Brasil\"). Cryptocurrencies have been also used to launder criminal activity and transfer financial assets to stooges.[[20]](#cite_note-20)\n\nOn January 24, 2023, two reports by Brazilian newspaper *[O Globo](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/O_Globo \"O Globo\")* detailed that the Bolsonaro government allocated [BRL](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazilian_real \"Brazilian real\") 872 million (approximately [USD](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/United_States_dollar \"United States dollar\") 171 million) from the federal budget to an opaque evangelical [non-governmental organisation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-governmental_organization \"Non-governmental organization\") from 2019 to 2023. According to indigenous leaders in the Yanomami territory the NGO has not worked in the region since it started receiving Bolsonaro administration [earmarks](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earmark_(politics) \"Earmark (politics)\"), raising suspicions of widespread [corruption](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corruption \"Corruption\").[[21]](#cite_note-21) Additionally, government funds allocated to transport doctors and nurses to the region during the Bolsonaro administration were directed to transportation companies owned by illegal miners, who were also reportedly tipped off about police raids and operations hours or days before they took place.[[22]](#cite_note-22)\n\nA *[Brasil de Fato](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brasil_de_Fato \"Brasil de Fato\")* report in February 2023 revealed illegal miners operating in the Yanomami territory had access to [Starlink](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/S\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 10000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
