{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_57","type":"mcp_tool_call","server":"fetch","tool":"fetch","arguments":{"max_length":8000,"url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigenous_peoples_in_Brazil"},"result":{"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contents of https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigenous_peoples_in_Brazil:\nNative Brazilians\n\n| *Brasileiros nativos*   ([Portuguese](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portuguese_language \"Portuguese language\")) | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Proportion of Native Brazilians in each municipality as of the [2022 Brazilian census](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Brazilian_census \"2022 Brazilian census\") | |\n| Total population | |\n| **Amerindian ancestry predominates** Increase **1,227,642** ([2022 census](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2022_Brazilian_census \"2022 Brazilian census\"))[[1]](#cite_note-censo2022-1) Increase 0.60% of the Brazilian population | |\n| Regions with significant populations | |\n| Predominantly in the [North](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northern_Region,_Brazil \"Northern Region, Brazil\") and [Central-West](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Central-West_Region,_Brazil \"Central-West Region, Brazil\") | |\n| Languages | |\n| [Indigenous languages](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigenous_languages_of_the_Americas \"Indigenous languages of the Americas\"), [Portuguese](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazilian_Portuguese \"Brazilian Portuguese\") | |\n| Religion | |\n| [Indigenous religion](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigenous_religion \"Indigenous religion\"), [animism](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Animism \"Animism\"). 61.1% [Roman Catholic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Catholic_Church \"Catholic Church\"), 19.9% [Protestant](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Protestantism \"Protestantism\"), 11% [non-religious](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Irreligion \"Irreligion\"), 8% other beliefs.[[2]](#cite_note-FGV-2) | |\n| Related ethnic groups | |\n| Other [Indigenous peoples of the Americas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigenous_peoples_of_the_Americas \"Indigenous peoples of the Americas\") | |\n\n**Indigenous peoples in Brazil** or **Native Brazilians** ([Portuguese](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portuguese_language \"Portuguese language\"): *Brasileiros nativos*) are the peoples whose ancestors lived in [Brazil](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazil \"Brazil\") before European contact around 1500 and those [pre-Columbian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pre-Columbian \"Pre-Columbian\") forebears. [Indigenous peoples](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigenous_peoples_of_the_Americas \"Indigenous peoples of the Americas\") once comprised an estimated 2,000 distinct tribes and nations inhabiting what is now Brazil. The 2010 Brazil census recorded 305 Indigenous ethnic groups.\n\nHistorically, many Indigenous peoples of Brazil were semi-[nomadic](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nomadic \"Nomadic\") and combined hunting, fishing, and [gathering](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hunter-gatherer \"Hunter-gatherer\") with migratory agriculture. Many tribes were massacred by European settlers, and others assimilated into the growing [Brazilian population](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazilians \"Brazilians\").\n\nThe Indigenous population was decimated by European diseases, declining from a pre-Columbian high of 2 million to 3 million to approximately 300,000 by 1997, distributed among 200 tribes. According to the 2022 [IBGE](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brazilian_Institute_of_Geography_and_Statistics \"Brazilian Institute of Geography and Statistics\") census, 1,694,836 Brazilians classified themselves as Indigenous,[[3]](#cite_note-3) divided into 391 ethnic groups and the census recorded 295 [Indigenous languages](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Languages_of_Brazil \"Languages of Brazil\").[[4]](#cite_note-census22-4)[[5]](#cite_note-5) Almost 77% of Indigenous Brazilians speak [Portuguese](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portuguese_language \"Portuguese language\").[[6]](#cite_note-iwgia-6)\n\nOn 18 January 2007, [Fundação Nacional do Índio](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Funda%C3%A7%C3%A3o_Nacional_do_%C3%8Dndio \"Fundação Nacional do Índio\") reported 67 remaining [uncontacted tribes](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Uncontacted_tribes \"Uncontacted tribes\") in Brazil, up from 40 known in 2005. With this increase, Brazil surpassed [New Guinea](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Guinea \"New Guinea\"), becoming the country with the largest number of uncontacted peoples in the world.[[7]](#cite_note-:0-7)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Parque_Ind%C3%ADgena_do_Xingu.jpg/250px-Parque_Ind%C3%ADgena_do_Xingu.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Parque_Ind%C3%ADgena_do_Xingu.jpg)\n\n[Xingu](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Xingu_peoples \"Xingu peoples\"), an [Indigenous territory of Brazil](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indigenous_territory_(Brazil) \"Indigenous territory (Brazil)\")\n\nQuestions about the original [settlement of the Americas](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Prehistoric_migration_and_settlement_of_the_Americas_from_Asia \"Prehistoric migration and settlement of the Americas from Asia\") have led to various hypothetical models. The origins of these Indigenous peoples remain a matter of debate among archaeologists.[[8]](#cite_note-AshRobinson2011-8)\n\n#### Migration into the continents\n\n[[edit](/w/index.php?title=Indigenous_peoples_in_Brazil&action=edit&section=3 \"Edit section: Migration into the continents\")]\n\nIn Brazil, most Native tribes living in the land by 1500 are thought to be descended from the first wave of Siberian migrants to the Americas, who are believed to have crossed the [Bering Land Bridge](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bering_Land_Bridge \"Bering Land Bridge\") at the end of the last Ice Age, between 13,000 and 17,000 years ago.[[9]](#cite_note-CordellLightfoot2008-9)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d0/Indios_apiaka_no_rio_Arinos.jpg/250px-Indios_apiaka_no_rio_Arinos.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Indios_apiaka_no_rio_Arinos.jpg)\n\n[Apiacá people](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apiac%C3%A1_people \"Apiacá people\"), painted by [Hércules Florence](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%A9rcules_Florence \"Hércules Florence\"), 1827\n\nAn analysis of [Amerindian](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genetic_history_of_Indigenous_peoples_of_the_Americas \"Genetic history of Indigenous peoples of the Americas\") [Y-chromosome DNA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Y-chromosome_DNA \"Y-chromosome DNA\") reveals specific clustering within much of the South American population. The [micro-satellite](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Microsatellite_(genetics) \"Microsatellite (genetics)\") diversity and distributions of Y-chromosome lineages specific to South America suggest that certain Amerindian populations have been isolated since the initial colonization of the region.[[10]](#cite_note-subclades-10)\n\nAccording to a 2012 [autosomal DNA](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autosomal_DNA \"Autosomal DNA\") genetic study,[[11]](#cite_note-nature.com-11) Indigenous peoples of the Americas descend from at least three main migrant waves from Siberia. Most of their ancestry traces back to a single ancestral population, referred to as the 'First Americans'. However, [Inuit-speaking](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Inuit_languages \"Inuit languages\") populations from the Arctic inherited nearly half of their ancestry from a second Siberian migrant wave, while [Na-dene](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Na-dene \"Na-dene\") speakers inherited about one-tenth of their ancestry from a third migrant wave. The initial settlement of the Americas was followed by a rapid expansion southward along the coast, with limited gene flow later, especially in [South America](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/South_America \"South America\"). An exception to this is the [Chibcha](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chibcha \"Chibcha\") speakers, whose ancestry includes contributions from both North and South America.[[11]](#cite_note-nature.com-11)\n\n[![](//upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Menina_da_etnia_Terena.jpg/250px-Menina_da_etnia_Terena.jpg)](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File:Menina_da_etnia_Terena.jpg)\n\n[Terena](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Terena_people \"Terena people\") girl at the closing ceremony of the Indigenous Peoples Games\n\nAnother study, focused on mitochondrial DNA ([mtDNA](htt\n\n<error>Content truncated. Call the fetch tool with a start_index of 8000 to get more content.</error>"}],"structured_content":null},"error":null,"status":"completed"}}
