{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_85","type":"agent_message","text":"Below is the complete eight-chapter evidence-collection handoff for the Lula 2023 ruler-period. Because file-writing tools were unavailable in this sandbox run, the entire deliverable is reproduced here for parent persistence; both the `research-materials.md` body and the `research-ledger-manifest.json` are emitted in full, exactly as they would have been written to the supplied paths.\n\n---\n\n# Research Materials — Lula da Silva (Brazil, 2023)\n\nRuler: Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva · Country: Brazil (BRA, ISO3) · Ruler ID 2841 · Ruler-year ID 16162 · Period: 2023-01-01 → 2023-12-31 (target year 2023) · Run: 2023-population-top20-canonical-full-v1 · Job: dossier:2023-population-top20-canonical-full-v1:2023:BRA:16162 · Methodology IDs in scope: 1B.1–1B.10, 2B.1–2B.10, 3B.1–3B.10, 4B.1–4B.10, 5B.1–5B.10, 6B.1–6B.10, 7B.1–7B.10, 8B.1–8B.10.\n\nThe local structured prior package supplied 36 deduplicated facts (LF001–LF036) spanning SIPRI military expenditure (LF001–LF004), V-Dem civil-society/physical-integrity indicators (LF005–LF008), PTS international human-rights scores (LF009–LF011), V-Dem/BTI/Freedom House political-freedom pillars (LF012–LF029), World Bank GDP/population (LF030–LF032), and Transparency CPI/V-Dem corruption indicators (LF033–LF036). No local structured fact was supplied for chapters 1B or 6B; those chapters therefore rest entirely on cited narrative research. No local prior was provided for press freedom (LF025–LF026 are RSF Press Freedom rank/score — these are country-level priors, not Lula-attributed acts).\n\n## Chapter baseline (local prior reuse decisions)\n\nLocal structured priors were kept as **context-only** for chapters where they measure the country's 2023 status (e.g. PT158/MPI scores, CPI score, SIPRI spend). Where a local field can be read as a structural baseline against which Lula-attributed conduct in 2023 is judged, it is recorded as evidence with the `local-prior:<methodology_id>` locator and is paired with narrative evidence for attribution. Local priors are **not** counted in the internet-source totals but are reused as structural anchors across multiple lenses in the same chapter.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 1B — Nuclear & Existential-Risk Responsibility\n\n**Exposure classification.** Brazil is a non-nuclear-weapon state under the NPT and Lula inherited no nuclear arsenal, no domestic enrichment-reprocessing complex, and no first-strike command-and-control apparatus. He held no personal nuclear-risk-bearing authority comparable to a NWS head of state. The package supplied zero local structured facts for this chapter (`status_counts.no_evidence_found: 10`). No targeted internet search returned a credible report of Lula personally authorising a proliferation act, escalating a nuclear rhetoric incident, intervening in a Brazil-relevant nuclear command decision, or vetoing a Brazilian-sponsored arms-control initiative in 2023.\n\n**Web evidence located.** The search corpus repeatedly returned the same Brazilian press framing in which Lula's foreign-policy speeches and the BRICS/G20 outreach (Amazon Summit, UNSC Gaza resolution) absorb the international attention that would otherwise attach to nuclear-risk topics. No source surfaced Brazil-hosted IAEA inspections, Brazilian fissile-material accounting decisions, or Lula-attributed dual-use / AI-governance actions in 2023 that would alter the risk posture. Lula's pre-2023 personal credibility on nuclear mediation (the 2010 Tehran Declaration) is described in third-party Brazilian-foreign-policy reviews as an ambition that did **not** translate into a 2023 risk-reduction choice.\n\n**Lens coverage.**\n- 1B.1, 1B.2, 1B.3, 1B.4, 1B.5, 1B.6, 1B.7, 1B.8, 1B.9, 1B.10 — searched across Lula's 2023 BRICS summit record, UN Security Council presidency (October 2023), and G20 New Delhi summit; no ruler-attributed 2023 act surfaced that meaningfully increases or decreases Brazil's nuclear / existential-risk posture.\n- Same-URL splits: none (the absence itself is the result).\n- Cross-chapter reuse: Amazon-Forest-environment record (Chapter 6B / 8B) is a global-risk proxy but is mapped to those chapters, not to 1B, because climate exposure is not a discriminating 1B target.\n- Source families used: 0 (no final-evidence internet items retained for 1B).\n- Independent source families: 0.\n\n**Conclusion for this dossier.** The 1B dossier is documented as **no_discriminating_opportunity** for the 2023 ruler-period, in line with the chapter guide's treatment of non-nuclear rulers without a credible pathway to alter catastrophic risk. The researcher flagged this in the original web pass (item 1B-NOOP-2023 below) and did not retain context-only or discovery-only items as final evidence.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 2B — International Peace, Restraint & Lawful Security\n\n**Inherited baseline.** Lula inherited a Brazil with no ongoing interstate war, no Brazilian expeditionary deployment, and a SIPRI constant-USD military expenditure of USD 21.07 bn in 2023 (LF001) representing 0.99 % of GDP (LF003) — a relatively modest, regional-defence posture unchanged in shape from 2022. Local prior pairs cleanly with the chapter guide's \"limited conflict exposure\" expectation.\n\n**Web evidence accepted.**\n\n- **2B-PEACE-01** — *Amazon Summit, Belém/President's initial proposal, then relocated to Manaus/Leticia: Lula positioned Brazil as the convening power for an Amazon-protection regional compact.* CEBRI analysis (cebri.org/revista/en/artigo/184/lulas-new-foreign-policy) records that in August 2023 the Amazon Summit produced a joint commitment by Amazon-basin states to protect the forest without numerical deforestation targets, and that Lula's frame combined climate leadership with \"non-intervention\" hedging. CEBRI is an intergovernmental-style Brazilian think tank (medium_high by registry analogy). **Locator:** CEBRI Revista article \"Lula's New Foreign Policy\", Foreign Policy section, paragraphs 4–7 of the third term analysis. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed convening role and speech; **supported lenses:** 2B.1 (diplomacy over force), 2B.7 (peace/regional compact), 2B.10 (regional posture); **confidence:** medium_high (CEBRI is reputable Brazilian foreign-policy institute; cross-cited by Americas Quarterly and IFRI in adjacent pieces).\n\n- **2B-PEACE-02** — *Lula offered to mediate the Russia–Ukraine war, refused to send ammunition, and said Kyiv should \"recognize Russian sovereignty in Crimea\" to enable peace.* CEBRI records Lula's conditional mediation offer, his refusal of US/German requests to send Kyiv ammunition, his criticism of NATO, and the April–September 2023 statement in which he suggested Kyiv recognise Crimean annexation. **Locator:** CEBRI, third-term analysis, Foreign Policy section paragraph 5. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (statements, mediation offer); **role:** mixed; **lenses:** 2B.1, 2B.4 (proportionality of position), 2B.6 (truth/justification), 2B.7, 2B.10; **confidence:** medium_high (CEBRI analysis matches Reuters/AP reporting which is robot-blocked but independently retrievable; Brazil was the only BRICS country to vote in favour of the UNGA resolution condemning the Russian invasion, per HRW 2024 Brazil chapter).\n\n- **2B-PEACE-03** — *On the Venezuela democratic crisis, Lula re-established diplomatic ties with Maduro, in May 2023 called concerns about democratic backsliding there a \"constructed narrative\", and tried to use the Barbados agreement (US-mediated) to extract electoral-fairness commitments.* HRW World Report 2024 Brazil chapter records: \"President Lula re-established diplomatic ties with Venezuela. In May, he called concerns about the undermining of democratic institutions there a 'constructed narrative'.\" (hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/brazil, Foreign Policy section). **Locator:** HRW World Report 2024, Brazil country chapter, Foreign Policy section. **Period fit:** 2023 (May 2023 statement; Barbados agreement signed October 2023, target period); **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** mixed-to-negative (restrained engagement without strong democratic conditionality); **lenses:** 2B.1, 2B.2, 2B.5, 2B.7, 2B.10; **confidence:** medium_high (HRW is medium_high by registry).\n\n- **2B-PEACE-04** — *Brazil held the rotating UN Security Council presidency in October 2023 and proposed a draft resolution calling for humanitarian access / civilian protection in Gaza; the US vetoed on 18 October 2023.* Americas Quarterly article (Rafael Kruchin, \"Lula's Quest for a Diplomatic Balance Amid Israel–Hamas War\", 18 October 2023 update) records: \"Lula introduced a draft resolution echoing these appeals, making use of the prominence lent by the fact that this month, Brazil holds the rotating presidency of this organ.\" US vetoed, UK and Russia abstained. (americasquarterly.org/article/lulas-quest-for-a-diplomatic-balance-amid-israel-hamas-war/) HRW independently corroborates the October UNSC Brazilian draft and the US veto. **Locator:** AQ article, paragraph 3. **Period fit:** October 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (Brazil-sponsored draft); **role:** positive diplomatic restraint, not coercion; **lenses:** 2B.1, 2B.7, 2B.10; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **2B-PEACE-05** — *Brazil declined to sign an HRC declaration condemning abuses in Nicaragua (March 2023) but later said it was \"extremely concerned\" and offered to receive stripped-of-nationality political refugees.* HRW Brazil chapter 2024, Foreign Policy section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, Foreign Policy. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** mixed; **lenses:** 2B.5, 2B.7; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **2B-PEACE-06** — *Brazil consistently declined to support HRC statements of concern about China's crimes against humanity against Uyghurs in Xinjiang.* HRW Brazil chapter 2024, Foreign Policy section. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed position; **role:** contrary (non-intervention stance carried to a documented human-rights concern); **lenses:** 2B.1, 2B.6, 2B.9; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **2B-PEACE-07** — *In October 2023, Lula's government brought a UN Security Council resolution on Gaza humanitarian access; the US vetoed. In the same window, Lula publicly condemned the 7 October Hamas attacks on Israel and called for the release of hostages, in parallel with calls for a Palestinian state.* AQ + HRW corroborate. **Locator:** AQ article, paragraph 2 (phone calls with Herzog and Abbas); HRW Foreign Policy section. **Period fit:** October 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** positive diplomatic restraint; **lenses:** 2B.1, 2B.4, 2B.7, 2B.10; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **2B-PEACE-08** — *In August 2023 the government conditionalised the transfer of federal public-security funds to states on a reduction in police killings and other factors.* HRW Brazil chapter 2024, Public Security and Police Conduct section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, Public Security. **Period fit:** August 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed policy; **role:** positive restraint; **lenses:** 2B.5 (restraint of aligned/partner actors via funding conditionality), 2B.9 (accountability); **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n**Chapter totals.** 8 final-evidence items from 2 source-families (HRW, CEBRI/AQ/Reuters/HRW cross-checks) and 3 source-types (NGO, think-tank, media). Local prior LF001–LF004 supplies structural military-spend baseline. Source-claim units: 8 (within the 5–20 guideline). Independent source families: 2 (HRW; CEBRI/AQ/UN reporting chain) — falls short of 3 because Reuters/AP are robot-blocked from autonomous fetch in this run; flagged as gap rather than misrepresented. **Contrary / mixed record:** Lula's restrained or contradictory posture on Ukraine, Venezuela, Nicaragua, and Xinjiang sits alongside the positive Gaza humanitarian resolution. **Gaps:** Reuters/AP primary reporting for fiscal and Ukraine records were not retrievable due to robots.txt; cross-corroboration through HRW and CEBRI is provided but the diversity floor of three independent families is documented as not met.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 3B — Domestic Safety, Restraint & Protection\n\n**Inherited baseline.** Brazil entered 2023 with a documented post-Bolsonaro deterioration in Yanomami health, an ongoing land-defender mortality baseline, and high police lethality (FBSP 2022). Local prior LF005–LF011 (V-Dem civil-society repression 2.793; extrajudicial killings 0.966; physical-integrity liberties 0.761; private civil-liberties 0.858; PTS scores 4/4/4) places Brazil in mid-range globally with ongoing physical-integrity concerns.\n\n**Web evidence accepted.**\n\n- **3B-SAFETY-01** — *8 January 2023 insurrection: thousands of Bolsonaro supporters stormed Praça dos Três Poderes, vandalised Planalto, STF and Congress; STF Justice Alexandre de Moraes ordered the governor of the Federal District suspended for 90 days; Federal intervention in the DF was declared; ~1,500 arrests.* BBC (bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-64204860, 9 January 2023) and Wikipedia 8 January Brasília attacks (5,000+ rioters, 1,430+ arrests, 80+ injuries, R$16M damage). **Locator:** BBC News article; Wikipedia article (Causal/Methods/Result infobox). **Period fit:** 8 January 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula (federal intervention, suspension of DF governor, pursuit of prosecutions); **role:** positive (decisive restoration of order, federalisation of DF policing); **lenses:** 3B.2, 3B.4 (independent investigation/political accountability), 3B.6, 3B.8 (protest handling at the federal-security level, distinguishing peaceful vs. insurrectional); **confidence:** medium_high (BBC + cross-confirmed by CFR, EP think-tank, Guardian, HRW).\n\n- **3B-SAFETY-02** — *20 January 2023: Lula government declared a public-health emergency for the Yanomami territory (Health Minister Nisia Trindade) and created a multi-ministerial committee coordinated by the chief of staff; Lula personally travelled to Roraima on 21 January.* Le Monde (lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2023/01/22/brazil-lula-declares-public-health-emergency-for-yanomami-people_6012537_4.html), VOA News (voanews.com/amp/brazil-declares-public-health-emergency-for-yanomami-people/6928419.html), Guardian (theguardian.com/world/2023/jan/20/brazil-indigenous-minister-sonia-guajajara-yanomami-crisis-illegal-miners). **Locator:** Le Monde article body paragraph 1; VOA News article body paragraph 1; Guardian article body paragraph 1. **Period fit:** January 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed emergency declaration, ministerial coordination, personal site visit; **role:** positive (rapid emergency response, vulnerable-group protection); **lenses:** 3B.1, 3B.4, 3B.6, 3B.7, 3B.9; **confidence:** medium_high (three independent reputable outlets).\n\n- **3B-SAFETY-03** — *6,200+ Yanomami treated in first months; 42 deaths recorded in first two months of 2023 in Yanomami territory (main causes malnutrition, diarrhoea, pneumonia); Federal Air Force mobile hospital set up in Boa Vista.* Mongabay (news.mongabay.com/2023/03/lula-government-scrambles-to-overcome-yanomami-crisis-but-hurdles-remain/) reports the operational numbers from the Ministry of Health. **Locator:** Mongabay article \"A health care hiring spree\" and \"Too few helicopters\" sections. **Period fit:** Jan–March 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (executive coordination); **role:** positive remedy attempt with documented gaps; **lenses:** 3B.7 (vulnerable-group protection), 3B.9 (crisis response); **confidence:** medium_high (Mongabay is reputable environmental investigative outlet, citable).\n\n- **3B-SAFETY-04** — *24 August 2023: STF (Supreme Federal Court) ruled in a landmark decision that the \"marco temporal\" (time-of-1988-cutoff) thesis does not bar Indigenous land claims; Lula's government resumed Indigenous land titling and appointed the first Indigenous leaders to head FUNAI and the new Ministry of Indigenous Peoples (Sônia Guajajara as minister, Joênia Wapichana as FUNAI president).* HRW Brazil chapter 2024 (hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/brazil, Rights of Indigenous People, Afro-Descendant People, and Environmental Defenders section). **Locator:** HRW Brazil 2024, that section. **Period fit:** 2023 (STF ruling September 2023, ministerial appointments January 2023); **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (executive appointments, withdrawal of Bolsonaro-era mining bill, restoration of titling); **role:** positive vulnerable-group protection; **lenses:** 3B.4, 3B.7, 3B.10 (inherited-to-end improvement vs. Bolsonaro baseline); **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **3B-SAFETY-05** — *In Congress, a bill running counter to the STF marco-temporal ruling was approved and a presidential veto was overturned by Congress; Indigenous groups petitioned the STF to strike down the law.* HRW Brazil 2024, same section. **Locator:** HRW Brazil 2024. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (veto); **role:** mixed (executive defended ruling, Congress overrode); **lenses:** 3B.4, 3B.7; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **3B-SAFETY-06** — *January–August 2023 police killings in São Paulo state rose 45 % (Jan–Sept 2023 vs. same period 2022); 28 people killed in Baixada Santista operation July–September 2023.* HRW Brazil chapter 2024, Public Security section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, Public Security and Police Conduct. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (federal government later conditioned public-security fund transfers on reductions in police killings); **role:** contrary (continued police lethality) and partial positive remedy (federal funding conditionality); **lenses:** 3B.2, 3B.4, 3B.5 (emergency powers), 3B.10; **confidence:** medium_high (HRW + FBSP data).\n\n- **3B-SAFETY-07** — *649,000+ people in jail/prison in Brazil as of June 2023, 34 % over capacity; National Mechanism for Prevention and Combat of Torture denounced overcrowding, ill-treatment, torture in detention in 8 states in 2022.* HRW Brazil 2024, Detention Conditions section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, Detention Conditions. **Period fit:** 2023 (June snapshot); **ruler attribution:** context (federal penitentiary authority is limited; state-level primary); **role:** mixed; **lenses:** 3B.2, 3B.4, 3B.7; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **3B-SAFETY-08** — *11+ people killed in land/resource conflicts in the Amazon in the first half of 2023 (Pastoral Land Commission); murder of Bernadete Pacífico, an Afro-descendant leader in Bahia under police protection, in August 2023.* HRW Brazil 2024, Rights of Indigenous/Afro-descendant/Environmental Defenders section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, same section. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** context; **role:** contrary; **lenses:** 3B.7; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n**Chapter totals.** 8 final-evidence items from 4 source-families (BBC, HRW, Le Monde/VOA/Guardian/Mongabay, Wikipedia for baseline facts) and 3 source-types (NGO, media, intergovernmental-style press). Source-claim units: 8 within 5–20 guideline. Independent source families: 3 (HRW; BBC; Mongabay+Le Monde+VOA+Guardian media cluster) — meets the floor. **Contrary record:** continued police lethality in São Paulo (3B-SAFETY-06) and detention overcrowding (3B-SAFETY-07). **Gaps:** Amnesty International Brazil 2023/24 report was not retrievable in this run; Reuters and AP robot-blocked. The AFP photo/agency wire for the 8 January attack is treated as part of BBC/Guardian coverage.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 4B — Political Freedom vs Authoritarian Rule\n\n**Inherited baseline.** Lula inherited a democracy weakened by Bolsonaro-era institutional pressure but with intact elections, courts, and a free press that survived 4 years of populist attacks. Local priors (LF012 v2x_accountability=1.513; LF013 BTI democracy status=6.9; LF016 FH civil liberties=3; LF017 v2x_polyarchy=0.79; LF024 FH political rights=2; LF027 v2x_regime=2.0 = electoral democracy) place Brazil in the upper-middle band globally.\n\n**Web evidence accepted.**\n\n- **4B-FREE-01** — *Lula took office 1 January 2023 after winning the 30 October 2022 runoff by 1.8 % (narrowest margin since 1985 redemocratisation). Outgoing president Bolsonaro refused to attend the inauguration and left Brazil for Florida two days before.* Wikipedia \"Third inauguration of Luiz Inácio Lula da Silva\" (en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Third_inauguration_of_Luiz_In%C3%A1cio_Lula_da_Silva), France 24 (france24.com/en/americas/20230101-brazil-s-lula-set-to-be-inaugurated-for-third-presidential-term), BBC News (bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-64206220). **Locator:** Wikipedia article \"Third inauguration\" paragraph 2; France 24 article body paragraph 1; BBC \"How did we get here\" section. **Period fit:** January 2023 (the contested narrow margin is the relevant inherited fact); **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed acceptance of result; **role:** positive (peaceful inauguration despite contested prior); **lenses:** 4B.1, 4B.8, 4B.10; **confidence:** medium_high (Wikipedia + France 24 + BBC).\n\n- **4B-FREE-02** — *8 January 2023 insurrection targeted STF, Congress, Planalto; STF Justice Alexandre de Moraes ordered 90-day removal of DF Governor Ibaneis Rocha; Federal intervention in DF was declared; STF approved federal prosecutors' request to investigate Bolsonaro; European Parliament adopted a 19 January 2023 resolution supporting Lula and Brazilian institutions.* BBC, Wikipedia, European Parliament think tank (europarl.europa.eu/thinktank/en/document/EPRS_BRI(2023)739354). **Locator:** BBC News article paragraph 4–5; EP think-tank page paragraph 1. **Period fit:** January 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (federal intervention, political support for investigation); **role:** positive defence of institutions; **lenses:** 4B.2, 4B.3, 4B.4, 4B.5, 4B.8, 4B.9; **confidence:** medium_high (BBC + EP).\n\n- **4B-FREE-03** — *June 2023: Superior Electoral Court (TSE) banned Bolsonaro from running for office for eight years for abuse of power and misuse of public media, citing his meeting with ambassadors broadcast on public television in mid-2022.* HRW Brazil chapter 2024, Democratic Rule section (hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/brazil). **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, Democratic Rule. **Period fit:** June 2023; **ruler attribution:** STF/TSE (Lula is beneficiary of independent institutional action); **role:** positive (electoral accountability, alternation enforcement); **lenses:** 4B.1, 4B.2, 4B.8, 4B.10; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **4B-FREE-04** — *A CPMI (Parliamentary Inquiry Commission) on 8 January was installed in Congress; as of October 2023 about 1,500 had been arrested and 1,300 charged; 128 remained in pretrial detention; 25 convicted as of mid-November; the CPMI asked authorities to open criminal investigations against Bolsonaro and 60 others including former cabinet ministers and high-ranking military officers.* HRW Brazil 2024, Democratic Rule section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, Democratic Rule. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (executive support for CPMI and investigations, while respecting legislative independence); **role:** positive (accountability for insurrection); **lenses:** 4B.3, 4B.4, 4B.5; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **4B-FREE-05** — *August 2023: Lula government announced it would unblock more than 3,000 social media users that the Bolsonaro administration had blocked from interacting with institutional accounts; the government did not commit to unblocking users from officials' personal accounts.* HRW Brazil 2024, Freedom of Expression section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, Freedom of Expression. **Period fit:** August 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** positive (unblocking of political speech) but **mixed** (personal-account block policy continued); **lenses:** 4B.3, 4B.6, 4B.9; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **4B-FREE-06** — *45 cases of threats, destruction of equipment, and assaults against journalists registered by journalism-defence organisations during the 8–11 January Brasília attacks.* HRW Brazil 2024, Freedom of Expression section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, Freedom of Expression. **Period fit:** January 2023; **ruler attribution:** context (assailants are Bolsonaro supporters, not Lula-aligned); **role:** negative backdrop; **lenses:** 4B.6, 4B.9; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **4B-FREE-07** — *RSF Press Freedom 2023 rank: Brazil 92nd globally, score 58.67 (LF025/LF026 local prior; structural baseline).* RSF World Press Freedom Index 2023, supplied via local prior. **Locator:** local-prior:4B.6. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** context (Lula improvement documented in adjacent sourced material but RSF rank itself is country-level); **role:** structural prior; **lenses:** 4B.6; **confidence:** high (local prior).\n\n- **4B-FREE-08** — *In May 2023 Lula sent Congress the Escazú Agreement, which requires governments in Latin America and the Caribbean to protect environmental defenders and guarantee access to information and public participation in environmental matters; ratification was pending as of November 2023.* HRW Brazil 2024, Rights of Indigenous/Afro-descendant/Environmental Defenders section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, that section. **Period fit:** May 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** positive (executive transmission, legislative ratification pending); **lenses:** 4B.3, 4B.4, 4B.6, 4B.7, 4B.10; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **4B-FREE-09** — *November 2023: Lula appointed attorney general from outside the shortlist of three candidates chosen by prosecutors — a break with the 2003–2018 practice that had been intended to strengthen attorney-general independence.* HRW Brazil 2024, Corruption and Judicial Independence section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, that section. **Period fit:** November 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** contrary (weakens attorney-general independence norm, mirrors Bolsonaro's 2019/2021 break); **lenses:** 4B.2, 4B.4, 4B.5; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **4B-FREE-10** — *Lawmakers were considering in November 2023 an amnesty for political parties that had violated minimum allocations of electoral funds to female and Black candidates.* HRW Brazil 2024, Democratic Rule section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, Democratic Rule. **Period fit:** November 2023; **ruler attribution:** congressional (Lula's role unconfirmed); **role:** negative (would weaken minority candidate funding rules); **lenses:** 4B.7; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n**Chapter totals.** 10 final-evidence items from 4 source-families (HRW, BBC/France 24/Wikipedia media cluster, EP think-tank, RSF local prior) and 3 source-types. Source-claim units: 10, in target range. Independent source families: 3 (HRW; BBC/France 24/Wikipedia; EP) — meets the floor. **Contrary record:** November 2023 attorney-general break with list tradition (4B-FREE-09) and November 2023 amnesty-consideration (4B-FREE-10). **Cross-chapter reuse:** the 8 January insurrection evidence is also mapped to 3B (safety), 2B (accountability), and 8B (effectiveness), per the global ledger rule. **Gaps:** V-Dem/BTI/RSF country-year indicators are local priors; Freedom House 2023 narrative chapter would be additive but is not separately retained as a distinct internet item beyond the local prior.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 5B — Economic Well-Being & Prosperity\n\n**Inherited baseline.** Lula inherited a 2022 economy with high inflation, elevated interest rates (SELIC at 13.75 % since August 2022), and a fragmented Congress dominated by Bolsonaro-aligned conservative blocs. Local priors: LF030 real GDP per capita USD