e inflation declined, SELIC began cutting in August 2023, and the unemployment rate dropped to historic lows; these outcomes are described as consistent with the Lula administration's announced framework, but cannot be cleanly attributed to Lula alone given global factors.* IFRI \"First Year of Lula\" (Jan 2024) and Edelman Global Advisory review (Feb 2024) — both partially retrievable from search snippets: \"the government initially projected a 1.3 % GDP primary deficit for 2023\" (IFRI); \"Haddad also established a zero-deficit goal in the 2024 budget, a remarkable feat that solidified his position as Finance Minister\" (Edelman). **Locator:** IFRI snippet from PDF (ifri.org/sites/default/files/migrated_files/documents/atoms/files/ifri_martin_brazil_first_year_lula_2024.pdf); Edelman PDF snippet (edelmanglobaladvisory.com/sites/g/files/aatuss676/files/2024-02/Brazil_New%20Government%27s%20First%20Year_Review%20and%20Outlook_POLITICAL%20ECONOMY.pdf). **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (fiscal framework, finance-minister continuity); **role:** mixed-to-positive; **lenses:** 5B.3, 5B.5, 5B.7, 5B.10; **confidence:** medium (IFRI and Edelman are reputable analyst houses; underlying primary numbers would be Brazil's IBGE and BCB, not separately fetched).\n\n- **5B-ECON-10** — *Local priors: LF030 (real GDP per capita 2023 USD 10,377.59), LF031 (GDP total USD 2.19 trillion), LF032 (population 211.14 million), LF033 (V-Dem political-corruption index 0.461), LF034 (CPI score 36), LF035 (V-Dem executive-corruption 0.561), LF036 (V-Dem public-corruption 0.242), LF015 (BTI status index 6.75).* These provide structural context for the inherited/exit economic baseline. **Locator:** local-prior:5B.* **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** structural context; **role:** context; **lenses:** 5B.1, 5B.5, 5B.10; **confidence:** high (local prior).\n\n**Chapter totals.** 10 final-evidence items from 4 source-families (HRW, Covington, CEBRI, IFRI/Edelman) and 3 source-types (NGO, law-firm/think-tank, analyst house). Source-claim units: 10, in target range. Independent source families: 4 — exceeds the floor. **Contrary record:** Cerrado deforestation up 34 %; Lula's plan to join OPEC+ as observer at COP 28 (5B-ECON-06, 5B-ECON-07). **Gaps:** Reuters and AP economic data are robot-blocked from autonomous fetch. Specific IMF Article IV conclusion for Brazil was not retrieved in this run; if a downstream formatter needs precise GDP growth/inflation numbers, the local priors (LF030–LF032) plus Covington/IFRI narrative are the strongest defensible substitutes.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 6B — Social Well-Being & Human Development\n\n**Inherited baseline.** Local prior package returned `no_evidence_found` for every 1B lens (correctly mapped) and **also for every 6B lens** — there are no supplied HDI/health/education/welfare structured priors. The dossier therefore rests on cited narrative research alone for 6B. Lula inherited a Brazil with the Yanomami humanitarian crisis (Section 3B evidence) and a recently-restored social-protection architecture (Bolsa Família was structurally preserved through the Bolsonaro years but redesigned).\n\n**Web evidence accepted.**\n\n- **6B-WELFARE-01** — *Bolsa Família was restored/expanded by Lula's 1 January 2023 executive orders; the minimum benefit rose to BRL 600, plus BRL 150 per child under 6; the program targets roughly 21 million families and is the centrepiece of the 2023 social-protection expansion.* Confirmed via France 24 inauguration article (france24.com/en/americas/20230101-brazil-s-lula-set-to-be-inaugurated-for-third-presidential-term, \"Pressing to-do list\" section) and Gov.br Planalto biography. **Locator:** France 24 article; Gov.br biography. **Period fit:** January 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** positive (welfare expansion; reach to vulnerable families); **lenses:** 6B.1, 6B.2, 6B.3, 6B.7, 6B.8; **confidence:** medium_high (multiple sources, primary