th documented overcrowding, unhealthy conditions, and ill-treatment; youth detention dropped 50 % since 2018 (FBSP).* HRW Brazil 2024, Detention Conditions section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, Detention Conditions. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** context (federal authority over detention is limited; state-level primary); **role:** contrary (overcrowding, ill-treatment documented); **lenses:** 6B.7, 6B.8, 6B.9; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **6B-WELFARE-09** — *Refugee-status rules simplified for Venezuelans (gender identity, sexual orientation, FGM); Brazil hosted 499,650 Venezuelans as of October 2023 and had granted refugee status to 53,307 by 2022.* HRW Brazil 2024, Migrants, Refugees, and Asylum Seekers section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, that section. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (simplified asylum procedures); **role:** positive (vulnerable-group protection); **lenses:** 6B.3, 6B.6, 6B.7; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **6B-WELFARE-10** — *Escazú Agreement transmitted by Lula to Congress in May 2023; ratification was pending as of November 2023; the agreement specifically protects environmental defenders.* HRW Brazil 2024, Rights of Indigenous/Afro-descendant/Environmental Defenders section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, that section. **Period fit:** May 2023 transmission; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** positive (durability, vulnerable-group protection); **lenses:** 6B.5, 6B.6, 6B.9; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n**Chapter totals.** 10 final-evidence items from 4 source-families (HRW; Le Monde/VOA/Guardian/Mongabay media cluster; France 24; Gov.br) and 3 source-types (NGO, reputable media, official primary source). Source-claim units: 10, in target range. Independent source families: 3 (HRW; media cluster; official) — meets the floor. **Contrary record:** detention overcrowding (6B-WELFARE-08); Bernadete Pacífico murder (6B-WELFARE-07). **Cross-chapter reuse:** Yanomami evidence and STF marco-temporal ruling are mapped across 3B and 4B as well. **Gaps:** Amnesty International Brazil-specific 2023 report was not retrievable in this run; UNICEF/WHO specific 2023 country reports are not separately fetched.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 7B — Personal Integrity & Honesty\n\n**Inherited baseline.** Lula entered his third term with a contested personal-integrity record: he was convicted in 2017 (Operation Car Wash, the \"triplex\" case), spent 580 days in prison, and had his convictions annulled by the Supreme Federal Court in 2021 on the basis that the Curitiba judge (Sergio Moro) had been biased (and Moro had later joined the Bolsonaro government). The BBC explains: \"Lula denied ever owning the apartment. His conviction was later annulled and he was cleared to stand for office again... Lula supporters... say the case against Lula was political. Their view was backed up when Brazil's Supreme Court ruled that the judge behind it... had been biased.\" (bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-64206220). The Lula-attributed framing in this inherited baseline is therefore contested; both the annulled convictions and the political-prosecution critique are documented.\n\n**Web evidence accepted.**\n\n- **7B-INTEG-01** — *The Lula government announced in November 2023 that it would appoint an attorney general from outside the three-name shortlist chosen by prosecutors — the first such break since 2018 (Bolsonaro broke it in 2019 and 2021) and the first break on Lula's side since 2003.* HRW Brazil 2024, Corruption and Judicial Independence section (hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/brazil). **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, that section. **Period fit:** November 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** contrary (weakens attorney-general independence norm that had been defended by Lula 1.0); **lenses:** 7B.5 (appointments), 7B.6 (independent scrutiny), 7B.7 (obstruction of accountability architecture); **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **7B-INTEG-02** — *The Lula administration launched aggressive enforcement against illegal gold mining in Yanomami territory (a personal priority), which targeted political-business networks that had profited under the Bolsonaro government, including former army general Augusto Heleno's reported authorisation of a convicted drug dealer's gold-mining operation in the Yanomami region.* Wikipedia \"Yanomami humanitarian crisis\" (en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yanomami_humanitarian_crisis), citing Folha de S.Paulo 16 January 2023. **Locator:** Wikipedia article, \"Illegal mining logistics\" section. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed enforcement; **role:** positive (anti-corruption, anti-network enforcement); **lenses:** 7B.5, 7B.7 (use of state power to address illegal extraction networks); **confidence:** medium_high (Wikipedia citing Folha).\n\n- **7B-INTEG-03** — *The Lula government signed the Escazú Agreement and submitted it for ratification in May 2023; the agreement specifically requires governments to protect environmental defenders and guarantee access to information.* HRW Brazil 2024, Rights of Indigenous/Afro-descendant/Environmental Defenders section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil. **Period fit:** May 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** positive (commitment to transparency); **lenses:** 7B.1, 7B.6, 7B.8; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **7B-INTEG-04** — *Lula's \"rebuild Brazil\" inauguration speech (1 January 2023) pledged to fight for the poor, racial and gender equality, zero Amazon deforestation, and to \"respond to hate with love, to lies with the truth, to terrorism and violence with the law\".* France 24 article, body paragraph quoting Lula. **Locator:** France 24 article, body paragraph 1. **Period fit:** 1 January 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed public commitment; **role:** context (sets a public-standard frame for integrity judgments); **lenses:** 7B.1, 7B.8, 7B.10; **confidence:** medium_high (direct quote, primary source).\n\n- **7B-INTEG-05** — *Lula's first-year economic appointments (Fernando Haddad as Finance Minister; continued professional cadres in macroeconomic roles) constituted a competence-led staffing choice, not a cronyist one.* Covington \"Brazil: Lula's Balancing Act\" 8 June 2023 (cov.com/en/news-and-insights/insights/2023/06/brazil-lulas-balancing-act-in-domestic-and-foreign-policy). **Locator:** Covington article \"Domestic Policy Priorities\" and \"Foreign Policy Priorities\" sections. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** positive (competence, professionalism); **lenses:** 7B.5 (appointments), 7B.9 (nepotism/favouritism avoidance); **confidence:** medium_high (law-firm analysis).\n\n- **7B-INTEG-06** — *Local prior LF033 (V-Dem political-corruption index 0.461), LF034 (CPI score 36), LF035 (V-Dem executive-corruption 0.561), LF036 (V-Dem public-corruption 0.242).* Structural country-year baseline; **not** evidence of Lula's personal conduct. **Locator:** local-prior:7B.* **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** structural context; **role:** context only; **lenses:** 7B.1, 7B.4, 7B.6; **confidence:** high (local prior).\n\n- **7B-INTEG-07** — *The Lula government reopened investigations into Bolsonaro-era falsified COVID-19 vaccine records, sale of foreign-gifts, and incitement of the 8 January attacks.* HRW Brazil 2024, Corruption and Judicial Independence section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, that section. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed enforcement; **role:** positive (anti-impunity, accountability for predecessor); **lenses:** 7B.6, 7B.7; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **7B-INTEG-08** — *The Lula government's first-year foreign-policy posture prioritised \"non-intervention\" and \"sovereignty\" principles that consistently deferred public criticism of allied governments (Venezuela, Nicaragua, China on Uyghurs).* CEBRI article (cebri.org/revista/en/artigo/184/lulas-new-foreign-policy) and HRW 2024 Brazil Foreign Policy section. **Locator:** CEBRI \"Third Term\" section paragraph 5; HRW Foreign Policy. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** mixed (restrained stance, but documented avoidance of public human-rights criticism of allies); **lenses:** 7B.1, 7B.8; **confidence:** medium_high (cross-confirmed).\n\n- **7B-INTEG-09** — *The Lula government unblocked more than 3,000 social-media users that the Bolsonaro administration had blocked from interacting with institutional accounts; the government did not commit to unblocking users blocked by officials' personal accounts.* HRW 2024 Brazil, Freedom of Expression section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil. **Period fit:** August 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** positive (correction) but partial (incomplete remedy); **lenses:** 7B.2 (admit errors and correct), 7B.8; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **7B-INTEG-10** — *CPMI (Parliamentary Inquiry Commission) on the 8 January insurrection was installed in Congress; the CPMI asked authorities to open criminal investigations against Bolsonaro and 60 others including former cabinet ministers and high-ranking military officers. STF approved federal prosecutors' request to investigate Bolsonaro. EP adopted a 19 January 2023 resolution supporting the investigation.* BBC; Wikipedia 8 January Brasília attacks; European Parliament think tank; HRW 2024 Brazil Democratic Rule section. **Locator:** BBC News article; Wikipedia infobox; EP think-tank page; HRW 2024 Brazil. **Period fit:** January–December 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (executive support for the CPMI and STF investigation); **role:** positive (accountability for predecessors); **lenses:** 7B.6, 7B.7; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n**Chapter totals.** 10 final-evidence items from 4 source-families (HRW; BBC/France 24/Wikipedia media cluster; CEBRI/Covington think-tank cluster; local priors) and 3 source-types. Source-claim units: 10, in target range. Independent source families: 4 — meets the floor. **Contrary record:** attorney-general shortlist break (7B-INTEG-01); partial unblocking of personal-account blocks (7B-INTEG-09); restrained foreign-policy candour on allies (7B-INTEG-08). **Cross-chapter reuse:** CPMI / STF investigation evidence is also mapped to 4B and 8B; the attorney-general shortlist is also a 4B lens evidence. **Gaps:** Reuters/AP not retrievable; Lula's personal asset declarations (TCU/Secretaria de Transparência) were not retrieved in this run — if the judge needs them, they would be additive. The annulment of the Curitiba convictions is documented as inherited baseline (BBC) but is **not** final-evidence for either direction.\n\n---\n\n## Chapter 8B — Effectiveness & Competence\n\n**Inherited baseline.** Lula entered his third term with a clear prior governing template (2003–2010 \"playbook\"), a defined program articulated at inauguration, and a Congress dominated by Bolsonaro-aligned conservative blocs (the \"Centrão\"). The Covington piece and the Lula inauguration speech jointly establish the program: macroeconomic stability via new fiscal framework + Bolsa Família social-protection expansion + tax reform + democratic consolidation + Amazon deforestation reversal + BRICS/G20 re-engagement. This is a **mostly_explicit** program basis (Covington June 2023 documents the three economic anchors explicitly; the inauguration speech and France 24 coverage document the social-environmental anchors).\n\n**Web evidence accepted.**\n\n- **8B-EFFECT-01** — *Lula's program was explicitly articulated on inauguration day: rebuild the country, fight poverty, racial/gender equality, zero Amazon deforestation, democratic consolidation.* France 24 article, body paragraph 1 (france24.com/en/americas/20230101-brazil-s-lula-set-to-be-inaugurated-for-third-presidential-term). **Locator:** France 24 article. **Period fit:** 1 January 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** strategic-clarity positive; **lenses:** 8B.1, 8B.2, 8B.10; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **8B-EFFECT-02** — *Covington's \"Balancing Act\" (8 June 2023) documents Lula's three economic anchors in detail and identifies the playbook structure (conservative fiscal + state-owned role + centre-right congressional coalition via budget earmarks).* Covington article (cov.com/en/news-and-insights/insights/2023/06/brazil-lulas-balancing-act-in-domestic-and-foreign-policy). **Locator:** Covington article. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** strategic-clarity positive; **lenses:** 8B.1, 8B.2, 8B.4, 8B.5; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **8B-EFFECT-03** — *First major legislative victory: new fiscal framework approved by Congress on 22 August 2023 (379–64 in the House of Deputies), within months of being introduced in March 2023.* Covington article (cov.com/en/news-and-insights/insights/2023/08/brazils-new-fiscal-framework-approved-by-congress). **Locator:** Covington article, Framework mechanics section. **Period fit:** March–August 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** operationalization positive, congressional-mobilization positive; **lenses:** 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.7, 8B.8; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **8B-EFFECT-04** — *Yanomami emergency: declared 20 January 2023 by Lula's executive; 6,200+ treated in first months; mobile hospital set up by FAB; coordination across ministries under chief of staff; 100+ health workers recruited.* Le Monde, VOA News, Guardian, Mongabay as above. **Locator:** Le Monde; VOA News; Guardian; Mongabay. **Period fit:** January–March 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** operationalization positive (rapid mobilisation on a major inherited crisis); **lenses:** 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.4, 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.7; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **8B-EFFECT-05** — *8 January insurrection response: federal intervention in DF, 1,500+ arrests, suspension of DF governor, STF-investigation request approved, CPMI established.* BBC; Wikipedia; HRW 2024 Brazil; EP think tank. **Locator:** as above. **Period fit:** January 2023 onward; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** operationalization positive (institutional crisis management); **lenses:** 8B.2, 8B.3, 8B.4, 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.9; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **8B-EFFECT-06** — *Amazon deforestation dropped 49 % Jan–Oct 2023 vs same period 2022 (preliminary official data; Climate Observatory).* HRW Brazil 2024, Climate Change Policy and Impacts. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, that section. **Period fit:** January–October 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (enforcement and reactivation of Amazon Fund; ministerial coordination); **role:** positive outcome (substantial target-related progress on Amazon, even if Cerrado worsened); **lenses:** 8B.6, 8B.7, 8B.8, 8B.10; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **8B-EFFECT-07** — *STF September 2023 marco-temporal ruling reversed the time-of-1988-cutoff barrier; Lula executive withdrew Bolsonaro-era mining bill, revoked encroachment-enabling policies, resumed Indigenous titling.* HRW Brazil 2024, Rights of Indigenous/Afro-descendant/Environmental Defenders section. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, that section. **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (executive actions); STF (independent); **role:** mixed (executive aligned with STF; Congress passed a counter-bill and overrode Lula's veto); **lenses:** 8B.3, 8B.5, 8B.6, 8B.8, 8B.10; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **8B-EFFECT-08** — *Cerrado deforestation rose 34 % Jan–Oct 2023; Lula government announced at COP 28 (December 2023) that Brazil plans to join OPEC+ as an observer; this contradicts the \"zero illegal deforestation\" pledge.* HRW Brazil 2024, Climate Change Policy and Impacts. **Locator:** HRW 2024 Brazil, that section. **Period fit:** Jan–December 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed (pledges and announcements); **role:** mixed-to-negative (signals of weak follow-through on parts of the program); **lenses:** 8B.7, 8B.8, 8B.10; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **8B-EFFECT-09** — *Lula offered to mediate the Russia–Ukraine war; refused to send ammunition; suggested Kyiv recognise Russian sovereignty in Crimea; the offer was not accepted by the warring parties.* CEBRI (cebri.org/revista/en/artigo/184/lulas-new-foreign-policy) third-term section paragraph 5. **Locator:** CEBRI article. **Period fit:** April–September 2023; **ruler attribution:** Lula-attributed; **role:** mixed (reveals a foreign-policy ambition that did not translate into outcomes; adaptation/correction not documented); **lenses:** 8B.1, 8B.8, 8B.9; **confidence:** medium_high.\n\n- **8B-EFFECT-10** — *Local prior evidence: LF030–LF032 (GDP/population); LF033–LF036 (corruption indicators); LF012 (accountability); LF028 (rule of law) provide structural outcome context for 2023.* **Locator:** local-prior:8B.* **Period fit:** 2023; **ruler attribution:** structural context; **role:** context; **lenses:** 8B.7, 8B.10; **confidence:** high (local prior).\n\n**Chapter totals.** 10 final-evidence items from 4 source-families (HRW; BBC/France 24/Wikipedia/Le Monde/VOA/Guardian/Mongabay media cluster; Covington/CEBRI think-tank cluster; local priors) and 3 source-types. Source-claim units: 10, in target range. Independent source families: 4 — meets the floor. **Morality-separation check.** This chapter's score is explicitly moral-neutral; the 2023 program (economic stability + social-protection + tax reform + democratic consolidation + Amazon reversal + BRICS/G20 re-engagement) is described in operational terms, and the chapter 8B judge will be expected to evaluate implementation and adaptation without reference to the moral desirability of the program itself (covenant enforced by the Chapter 8B rubric; reproduced in this handoff). **Contrary record:** Cerrado deforestation, OPEC+ observer plan, Ukraine mediation that did not land (8B-EFFECT-08, 8B-EFFECT