FECT-01\", \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20230101-brazil-s-lula-set-to-be-inaugurated-for-third-presidential-term::lula-program-articulation-1-jan-2023\", \"disposition\": \"final_evidence\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\": \"8B-EFFECT-02\", \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://www.cov.com/en/news-and-insights/insights/2023/06/brazil-lulas-balancing-act-in-domestic-and-foreign-policy::strategic-clarity-2003-playbook\", \"disposition\": \"final_evidence\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\": \"8B-EFFECT-03\", \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://www.cov.com/en/news-and-insights/insights/2023/08/brazils-new-fiscal-framework-approved-by-congress::fiscal-framework-implementation-august-2023\", \"disposition\": \"final_evidence\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\": \"8B-EFFECT-04\", \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://news.mongabay.com/2023/03/lula-government-scrambles-to-overcome-yanomami-crisis-but-hurdles-remain/::yanomami-operationalization-q1-2023\", \"disposition\": \"final_evidence\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\": \"8B-EFFECT-05\", \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://www.bbc.com/news/world-latin-america-64204860::8-january-crisis-management-2023\", \"disposition\": \"final_evidence\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\": \"8B-EFFECT-06\", \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/brazil::climate::amazon-deforestation-49-percent-drop\", \"disposition\": \"final_evidence\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\": \"8B-EFFECT-07\", \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/brazil::indigenous::executive-titling-and-stf-alignment-2023\", \"disposition\": \"final_evidence\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\": \"8B-EFFECT-08\", \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2024/country-chapters/brazil::climate::cerrado-rise-and-opec-observer-mixed-2023\", \"disposition\": \"final_evidence\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\": \"8B-EFFECT-09\", \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://cebri.org/revista/en/artigo/184/lulas-new-foreign-policy::ukraine-mediation-not-accepted-2023\", \"disposition\": \"final_evidence\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\": \"8B-EFFECT-10\", \"canonical_fact_key\": \"local-prior:8B.*::structural-outcome-context-2023\", \"disposition\": \"context\"},\n\n    {\"provisional_id\": \"REJECT-2B-REUTERS\", \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://www.reuters.com/world/americas/brazil-2023-economic-recovery-poverty-rate-drops-lula-2024-03-06/::robots-blocked\", \"disposition\": \"rejected\", \"reason\": \"Robots.txt disallowed autonomous fetching; HRW and Covington analyses carry the substantive claim independently.\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\": \"REJECT-3B-AMNESTY\", \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://www.amnesty.org/en/countries/americas/brazil/::amnesty-2023-report-unretrieved\", \"disposition\": \"rejected\", \"reason\": \"Not fetched in this run; HRW and BBC/Guardian coverage carry the same operational facts for the 2023 target period.\"},\n    {\"provisional_id\": \"REJECT-IFRI-PDF-FULL\", \"canonical_fact_key\": \"https://www.ifri.org/sites/default/files/migrated_files/documents/atoms/files/ifri_martin_brazil_first_year_lula_2024.pdf::full-pdf-binary-only\", \"disposition\": \"rejected\", \"reason\": \"Full PDF returned as binary; only snippet text recoverable from search-result excerpt; used only as background.\"}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\n---\n\n## Rejected candidates (recorded, with reasons)\n\n- **Reuters/AP 2023 Brazil economic and political reporting** — robot-blocked from autonomous fetch in this sandbox run; substituted by HRW 2024 chapter and Covington analyses, which carry the same operational claims. Documented as a coverage gap, not a substantive rejection.\n- **Amnesty International Brazil 2023/24 country report** — not fetched in this run; HRW and BBC/Guardian coverage carry the same operational facts for the 2023 target period.\n- **IFRI \"First Year of Lula\" full PDF** — returned binary content; only snippet from search-result preview is reliable, used as background.\n- **Brazil Central Bank / IBGE primary economic releases** — not separately fetched; the local priors LF030–LF032 plus Covington/IFRI/Edelman narrative carry the structural macro picture.\n- **Personal asset / asset-declaration records (TCU / Controladoria-Geral)** — not separately fetched; should be additive if the judge wants a tighter integrity lens-7B.4/7B.5 cross-check.\n\n---\n\n## Source diversity summary\n\n- **Independent source families used across the dossier (final-evidence):** HRW (ngo, medium_high); BBC / France 24 / Guardian / Le Monde / VOA / Mongabay / Wikipedia (media + reputable outlets, medium_high); Covington & Burling / CEBRI / Americas Quarterly / European Parliament (intergovernmental / law firm / think tank, medium_high); Gov.br Planalto (official, primary source for inauguration facts). Reuters / AP / Economist / FT were robot-blocked and substituted.\n- **Source types:** NGO (HRW); media (BBC, France 24, Guardian, Le Monde, VOA, Mongabay); intergovernmental/law-firm/think-tank (Covington, CEBRI, AQ, EP); encyclopedic / general reference (Wikipedia).\n- **Source diversity:** chapters 2B, 4B, 5B, 6B, 7B, 8B all reach 3+ independent families or close to it. Chapter 1B has 0 because of the no_opportunity classification. Chapters 2B and 7B note that Reuters/AP diversity is functionally approximated through HRW/Covington/IFRI/Edelman/Cebri cross-checks.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-chapter reuse map (no duplication of evidence IDs)\n\n- **8 January 2023 insurrection:** 2B-PEACE-08 (funding conditionality, positive restraint context), 3B-SAFETY-01 (operational safety response), 4B-FREE-02 (institutional defence), 7B-INTEG-10 (accountability architecture), 8B-EFFECT-05 (crisis management implementation). Single canonical fact key reused, no double-counting.\n- **Yanomami crisis (January–March 2023):** 3B-SAFETY-02, 3B-SAFETY-03, 6B-WELFARE-02, 7B-INTEG-02, 8B-EFFECT-04. Single canonical fact key reused.\n- **STF marco-temporal ruling (September 2023):** 3B-SAFETY-04, 3B-SAFETY-05, 6B-WELFARE-04, 8B-EFFECT-07.\n- **November 2023 attorney-general shortlist break:** 4B-FREE-09, 7B-INTEG-01.\n- **Escazú Agreement transmission:** 4B-FREE-08, 6B-WELFARE-10, 7B-INTEG-03.\n- **Foreign-policy restraint posture:** 2B-PEACE-03, 2B-PEACE-05, 2B-PEACE-06, 7B-INTEG-08.\n- **Local priors LF012, LF014, LF028, LF033–LF036:** mapped across 4B / 5B / 7B / 8B as structural context, with disposition `context`.\n\n---\n\n## Self-audit and strongest contrary evidence\n\n- **Strongest contrary evidence:**\n  - November 2023 attorney-general shortlist break (7B-INTEG-01 / 4B-FREE-09).\n  - Cerrado deforestation up 34 % vs Amazon down 49 % (5B-ECON-06 / 8B-EFFECT-08).\n  - São Paulo police killings +45 % Jan–Sept 2023 (3B-SAFETY-06).\n  - Lula's May 2023 \"constructed narrative\" comment on Venezuelan democratic concerns (2B-PEACE-03).\n  - Lula's April–September 2023 suggestion that Kyiv recognise Crimean annexation (2B-PEACE-02).\n  - Brazil declined to sign HRC statement on Nicaragua and consistently declined HRC Uyghur statements (2B-PEACE-05, 2B-PEACE-06, 7B-INTEG-08).\n  - Bernadete Pacífico murder of an Afro-descendant leader under police protection in August 2023 (3B-SAFETY-08, 6B-WELFARE-07).\n\n- **Weak / context-only items:** local priors (LF001–LF036) — all retained as context, none used as evidence of Lula's personal conduct; the CEBRI article's analysis of Lula's foreign-policy overstretch is treated as third-party analytical opinion rather than as a primary fact.\n\n- **Ruler-attribution discipline:** every final-evidence item has an explicit Lula-attributed channel — executive order, ministerial nomination under his authority, federal intervention, speech, or veto. The STF marco-temporal ruling and TSE Bolsonaro ineligibility are STF/TSE independent actions, recorded as institutional outcomes that Lula benefited from but did not author.\n\n- **Temporal fit:** every final-evidence item is dated to 2023 (specific month where the date is part of the claim). Items retrospectively used (the September 2025 Bolsonaro coup-plot conviction, the February 2024 ambassador recall) are NOT retained as 2023-period evidence; they are explicitly excluded.\n\n- **Source-confidence profiling:** every final-evidence item carries its source URL, publisher, publication date (where available), and an implicit confidence profile aligned with the supplied `source-confidence-registry.json`: HRW = medium_high (ngo, final-evidence, year-fit); BBC / France 24 / Guardian / Le Monde / VOA = medium_high (media, final-evidence); CEBRI / AQ / Covington / EP = medium_high (think-tank/law-firm/intergovernmental, final-evidence with the published-analysis caveat); Gov.br Planalto = medium_high for the inauguration transcript and Lula's published biography (official, primary, formal_fact_or_primary_legal_record); Wikipedia = medium_high for orientation/context, retained here as backstop for chronology only.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of chapter yield and ledger\n\n- **Chapter 1B (nuclear/existential):** 0 final-evidence items; `no_discriminating_opportunity` documented.\n- **Chapter 2B (peace):** 8 final-evidence items, 3 independent source families (one shy of the floor because Reuters/AP robot-blocked), 8 source-claim units.\n- **Chapter 3B (safety):** 8 final-evidence items, 3 independent source families (meets floor), 8 source-claim units.\n- **Chapter 4B (political freedom):** 10 final-evidence items, 3 independent source families (meets floor), 10 source-claim units.\n- **Chapter 5B (economic well-being):** 10 final-evidence items, 4 independent source families (exceeds floor), 10 source-claim units.\n- **Chapter 6B (social well-being):** 10 final-evidence items, 3 independent source families (meets floor), 10 source-claim units.\n- **Chapter 7B (integrity):** 10 final-evidence items, 4 independent source families (exceeds floor), 10 source-claim units.\n- **Chapter 8B (effectiveness):** 10 final-evidence items, 4 independent source families (exceeds floor), 10 source-claim units.\n\nTotal defensible source-claim units across final-evidence: **66** (chapters 2B–8B, excluding 1B's no_opportunity). Source concentration: HRW appears as the primary citation in 6 of the 7 chapters (24 of 66 items), which is disclosed rather than hidden. The HRW 2024 Brazil chapter is itself an annual synthesis drawing on local NGOs (Conectas, Instituto Socioambiental, Pastoral Land Commission, ABRAJI, FBSP) and international organisations (UN, OHCHR, IACHR) and on the agency's own field investigations; that derivative structure is what enables the cross-chapter reuse. Where a single HRW section anchors multiple lens claims (e.g. the Foreign Policy section), each lens is recorded as a separate source-claim unit because they are materially distinct claims, not a paraphrase of one paragraph.\n\n---\n\n## Local-write status note\n\nThe supplied `research_materials_path` and `research_ledger_manifest_path` could not be written directly from this sandbox run (sandbox filesystem did not permit `mkdir -p` or `ls` of the target directory via `exec_command`; no other file-write tool is available in this session). The full content above is the authoritative handoff and should be persisted by the parent at:\n\n- `/home/liorshtram/projects/leaders-db/research/runs/2023-population-top20-canonical-full-v1/jobs/418/attempts/004-d0be8fd8-4ae/research-materials.md` (this report, minus the manifest JSON block, which lives below)\n- `/home/liorshtram/projects/leaders-db/research/runs/2023-population-top20-canonical-full-v1/jobs/418/attempts/004-d0be8fd8-4ae/research-ledger-manifest.json` (the manifest JSON above)"}}
